      "created_at": "2026-03-12T08:23:44.812Z",
      "cooked": "<p>Plant Floor in ERPNext is a quiet feature. But a powerful one.</p>\n<p>Most ERP systems are great at reports. You open them, check what happened yesterday, and close them. Very few show you what’s happening <strong>right now.</strong></p>\n<p>That’s where ERPNext Plant Floor quietly stands out.</p>\n<p>It’s not a report. It’s not a dashboard you check later. It’s a <strong>live reflection of your factory floor</strong>, updated in real time.</p>\n<p>At a glance, you can see:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Which machines are running</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Which job is in progress</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Which workstations are idle, off, or facing issues</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Where setup is still needed</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>No digging through screens. Just a live view of what’s actually happening.</p>\n<p><strong>But what makes this feature truly meaningful is who it’s built for.</strong></p>\n<p>Not just owners or managers sitting in offices.</p>\n<p>But the people <strong>actually working on the machines.</strong></p>\n<p>Plant Floor works directly with Job Cards. Operators can start and stop work, update workstation status, mark issues, and even move or add stock — all from one screen. No deep ERP knowledge needed.</p>\n<p>So, while you’re sitting in the office, your factory team is updating the system directly from the floor. Everyone sees the same picture. No follow-up calls. No confusion. Less chaos.</p>\n<p>It feels less like <em>“using an ERP”</em> and more like <em>operating a factory with clarity.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> Manufacturing → Plant Floor</p>\n<p>Features like this remind us that good software is not just about what’s possible on paper. It’s about building tools that <strong>real people can actually use, in real environments.</strong></p>\n<p>ERPNext gets that.</p>\n<p>Have you explored Plant Floor in your manufacturing setup? Would love to hear real experiences, good or bad. <img src=\"https://discuss.frappe.io/images/emoji/twitter/backhand_index_pointing_down.png?v=15\" title=\":backhand_index_pointing_down:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":backhand_index_pointing_down:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"></p>\n<p>Team Satat| Offical Frappe Partner | ERPNext Implementers</p>",
